.TH HALSAMPLER "1"  "2006-11-18" "LinuxCNC Documentation" "HAL User's Manual"
.SH NAME
halsampler \- sample data from HAL in realtime
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B halsampler
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ INSTANCE ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sampler (9)
and
.B halsampler
are used together to sample HAL data in real time and store it in a file.
.B sampler
is a realtime HAL component that exports HAL pins and samples them into a ring
buffer every time its function runs.
.B stmakd
streams the ring over a WebSocket, and
.B halsampler
is a non-realtime program that copies the stream to stdout, where it can be
redirected to a file or piped to some other program.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-n " COUNT
instructs 
.B halsampler
to read
.I COUNT
samples from the FIFO, then exit.  If
.B \-n
is not specified,
.B halsampler
will read continuously until it is killed.
.TP
.B \-t
instructs 
.B halsampler
to tag each line by printing the sample number in the first column.
Numbers count the samples the sampler took since the first one printed, so
they skip exactly the samples that were lost.
.TP
.BI "\-R " FILE
creates
.I FILE
once the stream is subscribed.
.B stmakd
sends the stream header only after it has set this connection's read
position, so every sample taken from then on reaches
.BR halsampler .
A script that starts
.B halsampler
in the background and then the HAL threads waits for
.I FILE
in between.
.TP
.B INSTANCE
the
.B sampler
instance to read; the default is \fBsampler\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B STMAK_REST_URL
base URL of
.BR stmakd ;
the default is http://127.0.0.1:5080.
.SH USAGE
A
.BR sampler (9)
must first be loaded with
.B halcmd loadrt
or a
.B loadrt
command in a HAL file.  Then
.B halsampler
can be invoked to begin printing its data to stdout.  The stream starts with
the samples taken after
.B halsampler
connected.
.P
Data is printed one line per sample.  If
.B \-t
was specified, the sample number is printed first.  The data follows, in the
order that the pins were defined in the config string.  For example, if the
.B sampler
config string was "ffbs" then a typical line of output (without
.BR \-t )
would look like:
.P
123.55 33.4 0 \-12
.P
.B halsampler
prints data as it arrives until it is either terminated or has printed
.I COUNT
samples as requested by
.BR \-n .
Usually, but not always, data printed by
.B halsampler
will be redirected to a file or piped to some other program.
.P
The realtime sampler never waits for its readers.
.B stmakd
drains the ring independently of how fast the client reads and holds a
backlog of a few MiB, but if the stream falls further behind than the ring's
.B depth
holds, the oldest samples are lost.  Choose
.B depth
to bridge momentary stalls of
.BR stmakd .
Every lost stretch is reported on stderr as it happens, naming the number of
samples lost and the sample they preceded; with
.B \-t
the sample numbers skip them too.
.P
The data format for
.B halsampler
output is the same as for
.BR halstreamer (1)
input, so 'waveforms' captured with
.B halsampler
can be replayed using
.BR halstreamer .
The
.B \-t
option should not be used in this case.

.SH "EXIT STATUS"
If a problem is encountered during initialization,
.B halsampler
prints a message to stderr and returns failure.
.P
It returns success when it has printed
.I COUNT
samples (if
.B \-n
was specified), or when it is ended by SIGINT or SIGTERM, after writing out
everything it received.  It returns failure if any samples were lost: the
output then has a gap that a later reader cannot see without
.BR \-t .

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR sampler (9)
.BR streamer (9)
.BR halstreamer (1)

